  <h2>Why it exists</h2>

  <p>
    3D Tiles is the dominant format for streaming large geospatial 3D scenes, and its
    ecosystem is lopsided. Producing tilesets is a solved problem: several mature,
    independent tilers write static trees of <code>tileset.json</code> and glTF payloads.
    Consuming them is concentrated in a small number of clients, each welded to the
    ecosystem it was written for.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Serving is the hole.</strong> In practice a tileset is either a directory of
    files behind a static web server or a content delivery network, or it is a hosted
    subscription. There is no lightweight dynamic server for 3D Tiles — one binary, several
    sources, a cache you can swap or remove, deployable from a laptop to an edge worker.
    The two-dimensional map world solved exactly this problem years ago and has been the
    better for it. Nobody has done it in three dimensions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The second hole is native rendering. No mature Rust engine consumes 3D Tiles, and none
    consumes streamed terrain meshes draped with raster imagery to draw a globe. Tuile is
    both halves, because they are the same code seen from two ends: the traversal that
    decides which tiles a camera needs is the same whether it runs inside the renderer or
    inside the server.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in the box</h2>

  <div class="grid">
    <div>
      <span class="name">tuile-core</span>
      <p>
        The whole standard, render-agnostic, no async runtime, no HTTP client. Compiles to
        <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>, and CI enforces it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">GeometryStream</span>
      <p>
        The streaming protocol as a core trait, not a network API. Three bindings —
        in-process, over a socket, and plain HTTP pull — and one renderer that cannot tell
        them apart.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">tuile-wgpu</span>
      <p>
        The reference renderer, native and in the browser. It proves the core end to end
        and it is the first consumer of every seam.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">Planetary globe</span>
      <p>
        Quantized-mesh terrain and raster imagery assembled into the same tile tree the
        3D Tiles traversal already walks — one selection algorithm, two data shapes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">USD output</span>
      <p>
        A tileset region written out as USD text and USDZ packages, in pure Rust with no
        C++ dependency, plus a scene-index integration that streams tiles into an offline
        renderer.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">Bake and replay</span>
      <p>
        Freeze a camera path's worth of geometry into a single container, then render it
        again on a farm, deterministically, without touching the network.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The rules it is built under</h2>

  <p>
    These are not style preferences. Each one is load-bearing, and each one has cost
    something to learn.
  </p>

  <ol class="rule-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The core depends on no backend.</strong> No renderer, no async runtime, no
      HTTP framework, no filesystem on production paths. Input and output cross named
      traits — <code>TileFetcher</code>, <code>PrepareRenderResources</code>,
      <code>TileCache</code>. Wanting to add a heavy dependency to the core is the signal
      that an abstraction is in the wrong place.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The core compiles to WebAssembly.</strong> Checked on every change. It is the
      cheapest possible proof that rule one still holds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>All geospatial arithmetic is 64-bit until rebasing.</strong> Earth-centred
      coordinates run to about 6.4 million metres; 32-bit floats resolve roughly half a
      metre there, which is visible jitter. Positions are rebased onto a local origin in
      f64 and only then handed to the GPU as f32.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What crosses a wire is standard 3D Tiles.</strong> Geometry is serialised as
      glb — never a private binary format — so any conforming client can read what Tuile
      serves, and Tuile can read what any conforming producer writes. The decoded form
      exists only in memory, on the consumer's side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The project defines itself by the standard.</strong> Not against any product.
      The normative reference is
      <a href="https://docs.ogc.org/cs/22-025r4/22-025r4.html" rel="noopener">OGC 3D Tiles 1.1</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Someone holding a tileset out of a tiler or a photogrammetry pipeline who wants to
      serve it cleanly, from one binary, with a cache they choose.
    </li>
    <li>
      A Rust developer who wants a tileset or a terrain globe drawn inside their own
      wgpu application without embedding a C++ engine.
    </li>
    <li>
      Anyone who needs a geographic area as USD or USDZ — for an offline render farm, or
      for an augmented-reality viewer that reads that packaging.
    </li>
  </ul>

